 be able to stop \nin the same distance as on a dry road. Reduce \nspeed by about one-third (e.g., slow from 55 to \nabout 35 mph) on a wet road. On pac\n\n[3] Question: To help manage space on the sides of your vehicle, you should\nExcerpt: Avoid traveling too closely to others on the sides of your vehicle. They may decide to change lanes suddenly, or they may trap you when you need to change lanes.\n\n[4] Question: If the road surface is slippery you should avoid using ?\nExcerpt: Engine’s braking effect and speed retarders! When the road is slippery, avoid using the engine’s braking effect and speed retarders. These can lead to wheel skids, which can be very dangerous.\n\n[5] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: Commercial Driver’s License Manual –Modernized Testing System   \n \nSection 2 – Driving Safely Page 2-19  \nVersion: July 2017 \n \n \n                Figure 2.14 \n \n2.7.7 – Space Needed to Cross or Enter Traffic \nBe aware of the size and weight of your vehicle \nwhen you cross or enter traffic. Here are some \nimportant things to keep in mind. \nBecause of slow acceleration and the space large \nvehicles require, you may need a much larger gap \nto enter traffic than you would in a car. \nAcceleration varies with the load. Allow more room \nif your vehicle is heavily loaded. \nBefore you start across a road, make sure you can \nget all the way across before traffic reaches you. \n \n2.8 – Seeing Hazards \n \n2.8.1 – Importance of Seeing Hazards \nWhat Is a Hazard? A hazard is any road condition \nor other road user (driver, bicyclist, pedestrian) that \nis a possible danger. For example, a car in front of \n\n</sources>\nQuestion: If you're driving on a slippery surface, you should allow ? space ahead."}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
