ety latch by hand. When you’re inspecting the coupling device, make sure the latch is positioned correctly over the locking lever.\n\n[3] Question: what can missing lug nuts do?\nExcerpt: Lug Nuts Holds wheel on axle. \nLoose or missing lug nuts could result in \nthe loss of a wheel and lead to a crash. \nInspection:  \n1. Inspect that all lug nuts are present. \n2. Inspect that lug nuts are not loose and there are \nno signs of rust trails or shiny threads that may \nshow looseness. \n3. Inspect that there are no bent, broken, or missing \nstuds.\n\n[4] Question: When a vehicle is lightly loaded, it has poor traction making ? more likely.\nExcerpt: Wheel lockup more likely! When a vehicle doesn't have a lot of weight, the stiff suspension and strong brakes make for poor traction. That means you're more likely to experience wheel lockup.\n\n[5] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: Commercial Driver’s License Manual – Modernized Testing System   \n \nSection 11 - Vehicle Inspection   Page 11-7 \nVersion: March 2025  \nRims \nDescription: The metal rims or wheels on which tires \nare mounted. \nWhy Inspect: Damaged rims can result in loss of a tire \nfrom the rim, loss of a wheel from an axle, loss of air \npressure in a tire, a wheel rolling off a rim due to \ndamage to flange, or split rim. A damaged wheel can \nresult in loss of vehicle control, leading to a crash. \nWheels and rims should be inspected for conditions \nthat could result in a complete or partial wheel \nseparation or air loss in the tire. \nInspection:  \n Inspect for damaged, cracked, or bent rims. Rims \ncannot have welding repairs. Inspect for rust \ntrails that may indicate rim is loose on a wedge \nwheel, if applicable.  \n Inspect bolt holes to make sure they are not \nelongated (out of round).  \nLug Nuts \nDes\n</sources>\nQuestion: Failure to keep the fifth-wheel plate properly greased could lead to"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
