ant added even when the \nengine is at operating temperature. \nNever remove the radiator cap or any part of the \npressurized system until the system has cooled. \nSteam and boiling water can spray\n\n[3] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: pply the brakes to be sure they work well. If not, \ndry them out further as described above. \n(CAUTION: Do not apply too much brake pressure \nand accelerator at the same time, or you can \noverheat brake drums and linings. \n \n2.14 – Driving in Very Hot Weather \n2.14.1 – Vehicle Checks \nDo a normal Vehicle inspection, but pay special \nattention to the following items. \n \nTires. Check the tire mounting and air pressure. \nInspect the tires every two hours or every 100 \nmiles when driving in very hot weather. Air \npressure increases with temperature. Do not let air \nout or the pressure will be too low when the tires \ncool off. If a tire is too hot to touch, remain stopped \nuntil the tire cools off. Otherwise the tire may blow \nout or catch fire. \n \nEngine Oil. The engine oil helps keep the engine \ncool, as well as lubricating it. Make sure there is \nenough engine oil. If you have an oil tempe\n\n[4] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: 14-16\n6. Hot and Cold\nSummer driving has its own perils. Truck drivers should especially be on the lookout for:\na. Lost, fatigued motorists on vacation who may suddenly stop, or swerve across several lanes of traffic to an\nexit.\nb. Overloaded cars with poor visibility and/or drivers distracted by kids and pets.\nc. Highway construction projects where roads suddenly turn into narrow lanes with confusing signs. Heavy\nequipment and pedestrians are often nearby.\nWinter ice and snow mean gearing down on grades to avoid wheel-spinning and brake lock-up which can lead to \njackknifing. If you find a traffic jam and multiple vehicle accidents, stay back and wait for them to clear before \ntrying to get your rig through. \n7. Attitude\nA good attitude is a professional truck driver’s badge of honor. Sharing the roads with automobiles must be a safety\nconcern for the professional driver.\nTEST YOUR KNOW\n\n[5] Question: While driving in very hot temperatures you should ? your tires.\nExcerpt: inspect your tires every 2 hours or 100 miles.\n</sources>\nQuestion: Why should a driver avoid removing a radiator cap while the engine is hot?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
