n of turning the wheel back in the other direction once you've cleared a hazard or traffic emergency.\nExcerpt: Countersteering.  When you countersteer, you are redirecting the vehicle back to the opposite direction you steered while trying to avoid a hazard or whatever else was in your path. This is part of the single driving action of steering and countersteering.\n\n[4] Question: You should use the ? valve only to test your trailer brakes.\nExcerpt: The trailer hand valve is also called the trolley valve or the johnson bar. Only use it to test your trailer brakes. To actually use them, use the foot brake, or you’ll risk skidding or jackknifing.\n\n[5] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: Commercial Driver’s License Manual – Modernized Testing System   \nSection 9 - Hazardous Material Page 9-4  \nVersion: July 2017 \n9.3.3 – Lists of Regulated Products \n \nPlacards. Placards are used to warn others of \nhazardous materials. Placards are signs put on the \noutside of a vehicle and on bulk packages, which \nidentify the hazard class of the cargo. A placarded \nvehicle must have at least four identical placards. \n \nThey are put on the front, rear, and both sides of \nthe vehicle. See Figure 9.3. Placards must be \nreadable from all four directions. They are at least \n250mm 9.84 inches square, square-on-point, in a \ndiamond shape. Cargo tanks and other bulk \npackaging display the identification number of their \ncontents on placards or orange panels or white \nsquare-on-point displays that are the same size as \nplacards. \n \n \n \nExamples of HAZMAT Placards \nFigure 9.3 \n \nIdentification nu\n</sources>\nQuestion: What is hydroplaning?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
