 Fire Prevention \nPay attention to the following: \nVehicle Inspection. Make a complete inspection \nof the \n\n[2] Question: most effective plan of action to take if your vehicle catches fire while you are driving?\nExcerpt: If your vehicle catches fire while you are driving, the most effective plan of action to take is:\n\n1) Pull over safely in an open area, away from buildings, other vehicles, and anything that can burn.\n\n2) Stop, set the parking brake, and shut off the engine.\n\n3) Get everyone out and move well away from the vehicle, upwind and away from traffic.\n\n4) Call 911.\n\n5) Only attempt to extinguish a small, contained fire if you have the proper extinguisher and a clear escape route. Aim at the base of the flames.\n\n6) Keep the hood closed for an engine fire. If the fire grows or involves fuel, retreat and wait for firefighters.\n\nIt is important to follow these steps carefully, as they are designed to help prevent further injury or death in the event of a vehicle catching fire while you are driving.\n\n[3] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: Commercial Driver’s License Manual –Modernized Testing System   \n \nSection 2 – Driving Safely Page 2-40  \nVersion: July 2017 \n \n \nClass/Type of Fires \nClass Type \nA Wood, Paper, Ordinary Combustibles \nExtinguish by Cooling and \nQuenching Using Water or Dry \nChemicals \nB Gasoline, Oil, Grease, Other Greasy \nLiquids \nExtinguish by Smothering, Cooling \nor Heat Shielding using carbon \nDioxide or Dry Chemicals  \nC Electrical Equipment Fires \nExtinguish with Non-conducting \nAgents such as Carbon Dioxide or \nDry Chemicals. DO NOT USE \nWATER. \nD Fires in Combustible Metals \nExtinguish by Using Specialized \nExtinguishing Powders \nFigure 2.20 \n \nClass of Fire/Type of Extinguisher \nClass of Fire Fire Extinguisher Type \nB or C Regular Dry Chemical \nA, B, C, or D Multi-Purpose Dry Chemical \nD Purple K Dry Chemical \nB or C KCL Dry Chemical \nD Dry Powder Special \nCompound \nB or C Carbon Dioxide (Dry) \n\n\n[4] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt:  for firefighters. \nPosition yourself upwind. Let the wind carry the \nextinguisher to the fire. \nContinue until whatever was burning has been \ncooled. Absence of smoke or flame does not mean \nthe fire cannot restart.\n\n[5] Question: Which of the following pieces of emergency equipment is required on every commercial motor vehicle A) tire chains, B) a list of emergency contact phone numbers, C) an accident reporting kit, or D) a fire extinguisher?\nExcerpt: A charged fire extinguisher! Each vehicle is required to have spare electrical fuses, 3 reflective triangles, 6 fuses or 3 liquid burning flares, and a charged fire extinguisher.\n</sources>\nQuestion: Where should a driver aim a fire extinguisher when fighting a small vehicle fire?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
