2026-10-18 10:39:15,029 INFO backend.app: llm final response topic=CDLA text=Bridges are typically made of materials that do not readily conduct heat away from their surface. This means that the temperature of a bridge's surface will tend to remain higher than the surrounding air, which is why you might see ice forming on a bridge's surface before you see ice forming on nearby roads.
It's worth noting that the specific reasons for why bridges tend to form ice before nearby roads are likely to vary depending on a number of different factors such as the specific materials used to construct the bridge, the specific temperature and humidity conditions present in the surrounding air, and so on.
