2026-10-18 12:29:32,636 INFO backend.app: llm final response topic=CDLA text=The reason why a tank can reach its weight limit before reaching its volume capacity is due to the different ways in which weight and volume are measured.
Weight is typically measured using scales, which measure the force applied to an object. The amount of weight that a tank can hold depends on the size and shape of the tank, as well as any additional features or equipment that may be installed in the tank.
Volume, on the other hand, is typically measured using graduated cylinders, which measure the volume of a liquid within a given container. The amount of volume that a tank can hold depends on the size and shape of the tank, as well as any additional features or equipment that may be installed in the tank.
In summary, the reason why a tank can reach its weight limit before reaching its volume capacity is due to the different ways in which weight and volume are measured.
