distance on the open road.\nc. Increases the distance of the gap to 6-8 seconds in bad weathe\n\n[2] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: Commercial Driver’s License Manual –Modernized Testing System   \n \nSection 2 – Driving Safely Page 2-17  \nVersion: July 2017 \n \nyou'd need 5 seconds for a 40-foot vehicle and 7 \nseconds for a 60-foot vehicle. See Figure 2.12. \n \nTo know how much space you have, wait until the \nvehicle ahead passes a shadow on the road, a \npavement marking, or some other clear landmark. \nThen count off the seconds like this: \"one \nthousand- and-one, one thousand-and-two\" and so \non, until you reach the same spot. Compare your \ncount with the rule of one second for every ten feet \nof length.  \nIf you are driving a 40-foot truck and only counted \nup to 2 seconds, you're too close. Drop back a little \nand count again until you have 4 seconds of \nfollowing distance (or 5 seconds, if you're going \nover 40 mph). After a little practice, you will know \nhow far back you should be. Remember to add 1 \nsecond for sp\n\n[3] Question: While driving a truck with a manual transmission, you're forces to stop on the right shoulder of an uphill graade,  When your ready to reenter traffic, what strategy will help you put the vehicle back in motion?\nExcerpt: If you are stopped on an uphill grade, use the parking brake to keep your vehicle steady until you can engage the clutch and begin to move forward.\n\n[4] Question: What is the safest way to back a commercial vehicle when a helper is available?\nExcerpt: When you have to back, here are a few \nsimple safety rules: \n- Start in the proper position. \n- Look at your path. \n- Use mirrors on both sides. \n- Back slowly. \n- Back and turn toward the driver's side \nwhenever possible. \n- Use a helper whenever possible.\n\nAgree on signals, keep the helper in sight, and stop immediately if visual contact is lost.\n\n[5] Question: Why should a driver avoid waving another motorist past the truck?\nExcerpt: The driver may not see every hazard, and the signal could encourage an unsafe pass.\n</sources>\nQuestion: What should a driver do when a tailgater follows the truck?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
