red light. A \nbuzzer may also come on. \n \nAnother type of warning is the\n\n[3] Question: In a straight truck, spring brakes will bring you to a stop if the air pressure drops between ? PSI\nExcerpt: In general, spring brakes will bring a straight truck or tractor to a stop when the air pressure drops between 20 and 45 psi. However, you shouldn't wait for this; once your low air pressure warning comes on, try to stop right away while you can still control the brakes.\n\n[4] Question: If you have a major leak in the service line of your air brakes, the air pressure will\nExcerpt: Lower the tank pressure rapidly.  It’s likely that you won’t notice a leak in the service line until you apply the brakes. When you do, the loss in air pressure from the leak will lower the pressure in the air tank pretty quickly.\n\n[5] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: en the brake lining pads of a caliper, \nsimilar to a large c-clamp. \n \nWedge brakes and disc brakes are less common \nthan s-cam brakes. \n \n5.1.9 – Supply Pressure Gauges \nAll vehicles with air brakes have a pressure gauge \nconnected to the air tank. If the vehicle has a dual \nair brake system, there will be a gauge for each \nhalf of the system. (Or a single gauge with two \nneedles.) Dual systems will be discussed later. \nThese gauges tell you how much pressure is in the \nair tanks.\n</sources>\nQuestion: What can increasing application pressure for similar stops indicate?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
