rk), it may result in an automatic failure for an \nunsafe act.  \nOn a bus, if you walk to the back \n\n[2] Question: While operating your bus or passenger vehicle, you must keep the emergency exit door in the ? position.\nExcerpt: You should always drive with your emergency exit door closed, and be sure that there’s an “Emergency Exit” sign clearly visible to passengers. At night, turn on the red emergency door light if your vehicle is equipped with one.\n\n[3] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: l forward when you \nleave. When you have to back, here are a few \nsimple safety rules: \n Start in the proper position. \n Look at your path. \n Use mirrors on both sides. \n Back slowly. \n Back and turn toward the driver's side \nwhenever possible. \n Use a helper whenever possible. \n These rules are discussed in turn below.\n\n[4] Question: After coupling you tractor-semitrailer, you need to make sure the air lines aren't crossed. Apply the trailer brakes and listen for ?\nExcerpt: You should be able to hear air escaping when you apply the brakes. To check for crossed air lines, you should shut off your engine so you can hear better. Apply and release the brakes, and listen. You should be able to hear air escaping when you apply the brakes.\n\n[5] Question: CDLA\nExcerpt: Commercial Driver’s License Manual –Modernized Testing System   \n \nSection 2 – Driving Safely Page 2-32  \nVersion: July 2017 \n \nReduce Speed. Speed must be reduced in \naccordance with your ability to see approaching \ntrains in any direction, and speed must be held to a \npoint which will permit you to stop short of the \ntracks in case a stop is necessary. \n \nDon't Expect to Hear a Train. Trains may not or \nare prohibited from sounding horns when \napproaching some crossings.  Public crossings \nwhere trains do not sound horns should be \nidentified by signs.  Noise inside your vehicle may, \nalso prevent you from hearing the train horn until \nthe train is dangerously close to the crossing. 