tick. This ensures that the engine is properly lubricated.\n\n2. Cooling system/coolant level by using the sight glass or dipstick (s). This ensures that the engine is properly cooled.\n\n3. Power steering fluid level by using the dipstick. This ensures that the power steering system is properly lubricated.\n\n4. Brake fluid  (hydraulic brakes only) level by using the dipstick. This ensures that the hydraulic braking system is properly lubricated.\n\n5. Windshield Washer Fluid.\n\nBy checking these fluid levels, drivers can ensure that their engine, cooling system, power steering system, and hydraulic braking system are properly lubricated and functioning correctly.\n\n[4] Question: Which fluid levels should a driver check during an engine-compartment inspection?\nExcerpt: The driver should check the following fluid levels during an engine-compartment inspection:\n\n1. Engine oil level by using the dipstick.\n2. Cooling system/coolant level by using the sight glass or dipstick (s).\n3. Power steering fluid level by using the dipstick.\n4. Brake fluid (hydraulic brakes only) level by using the dipstick.\n5. Windshield-washer fluid\n\nIt is important to note that these levels should be checked during an engine-compartment inspection, as they can indicate potential issues with the engine and its components.\n\n[5] Question: Be sure to check the  A) Power steering fluid, B) the temperature gauge, C) hydraulic Brake or D) The brake chamber?\nExcerpt: Before any trip, check the power steering fluid level by using the dipstick. The level needs to be above the refill mark.\n</sources>\nQuestion: What fluids must be checked on a pre?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
