ed to add one additional second. This situation would require a minimum of 5 seconds of space ahead.\n\n[4] Question: What fluids must be checked on a pre?\nExcerpt: During an engine-compartment inspection, drivers should check the following fluid levels:\n\n1. Engine oil level by using the dipstick. This ensures that the engine is properly lubricated.\n\n2. Cooling system/coolant level by using the sight glass or dipstick (s). This ensures that the engine is properly cooled.\n\n3. Power steering fluid level by using the dipstick. This ensures that the power steering system is properly lubricated.\n\n4. Brake fluid  (hydraulic brakes only) level by using the dipstick. This ensures that the hydraulic braking system is properly lubricated.\n\nBy checking these fluid levels, drivers can ensure that their engine, cooling system, power steering system, and hydraulic braking system are properly lubricated and functioning correctly.\n6 retrieved excerpts · inspect sources\nYOU\nFluids must be checked on a pre-trip with inspection.\nKNOWLEDGE ASSISTANT\nDuring an engine-co\n\n[5] Question: Be sure to check the ? fluid level during your pre-trip inspection of the engine compartment.\nExcerpt: Before any trip check these during your engine compartment pre-trip:\n- Engine oil fluid level by using the dipstick\n- Engine coolant. The level needs to be above the refill mark.\n- Power steering fluid level by using the dipstick. The level needs to be above the refill mark.\n- Windshield washer fluid\n- Brake fluid, if equipped with hydraulic brakes\n- Transmission fluid, if a dipstick is provided; follow the manufacturer’s checking procedure\n\nNever remove a coolant pressure cap while the engine is hot.\n</sources>\nQuestion: How long does it take a 40 foot truck traveling 50 miles per hour to stop?"}], "stream": true, "options": {"temperature": 0.2, "num_ctx": 16384, "num_predict": 2048}}
